WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.520
- [Narrator] This is a
strip of Arizona Highway,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.690
five years ago.

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.150
These rooftops are warehouses.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.030
Then two years ago, more warehouses.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.713
And here's that area today.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.380
On this 17 mile stretch of road,

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:17.700
there are now close to 40
warehouses with more coming soon.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.340
This strip has become one of
the fastest growing markets

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.320
for industrial real estate in the country,

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.450
as companies look to
shift how they move goods

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.850
to avoid supply chain bottlenecks.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.560
But why are companies picking this

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.960
desert lined highway specifically?

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.770
- To my right would be the new
Walmart Distribution Center.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.190
- [Narrator] Amazon, REI, Dick
Sporting Goods, UPS and more

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.890
fill out this strip known as Loop 303,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.960
which sits about 20 miles west of Phoenix,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:43.980
and follows an arc

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.010
that connects Interstate
10 to Interstate 17.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.230
But much of the development
is happening here

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.430
between I-10 and US-60, an
area that was once farmland.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:57.990
- Everything from cotton to
other types of feed stock

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.030
were were grown out here.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.300
And over time the land
became increasingly valuable

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.310
and so many of these farmers would sell

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.500
to industrial developers
or residential developers.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:08.333
And they'd come in

00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:09.900
and build the kind of
product you see today.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.420
- [Narrator] That product,
massive warehouse spaces

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.150
like this building that will
soon be a distribution center

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:16.843
for sportswear brand Puma.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:19.980
- - We are trying to be
prepared for the future

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.250
and therefore we decided to go to Arizona,

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.570
and we will build there 1.1
million square foot facility.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:28.920
- [Narrator] Companies
through the second quarter

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.470
had leased a net total
of 16 million square feet

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.930
of industrial space in
the greater Phoenix area.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.750
That put it up there with
Chicago and Dallas Fort Worth

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.430
as one of the country's busiest sites

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.650
for new logistics activity this year.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:41.779
Many of these buildings were built

00:01:41.779 --> 00:01:43.740
before they even had a tenant,

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.780
including Puma's future space.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.180
- Developers are confident
that there are more companies

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.100
that wanna locate along this corridor.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.920
And they're saying,
"We're gonna make a bet

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:54.630
that this is gonna pay off

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.430
if we build more warehouse space."

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.620
- [Narrator] Another
19.8 million square feet

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.900
of industrial space is
now under construction

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.560
in the Phoenix region which
includes the Loop 303 corridor.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.330
To see why companies are doing this,

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.090
look about 300 miles west
where there is a bottleneck

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.070
at the West Coast traditional freight hub,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.710
Southern California's Inland Empire.

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.840
Many of the shipments from
the ports of Los Angeles

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.720
and Long Beach stop here
after leaving the docks.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.940
But space here is tight.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.020
- In the Inland Empire,

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.790
the warehousing market has a vacancy rate

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.500
that's fallen below 1%.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.300
So that has forced companies

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.580
that otherwise would've
been leasing space there

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.080
to look for alternatives.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.550
- [Narrator] Before picking the 303,

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.860
Puma considered adding
more space in Torrance

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.560
where it has its existing
Southern California facility.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.850
But there was a problem.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.850
- There is no space available
right now in Torrance

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.830
and you have to look into the future

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.040
and I don't expect it's
getting better out there.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.350
- [Narrator] So for Puma,
that meant looking east,

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.090
but not too far east.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.490
The company said about 95% of its product

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.920
is imported from Asia,
so it needed a facility

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.920
that was still within driving
distance of the ports.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.890
- You have six, eight hours
to the time in transit.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.400
You go overnight anyway so I
don't think it's a big deal.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.160
- [Narrator] Trucking goods
over 350 miles to Phoenix

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.760
versus 15 miles to
Torrance can carry a cost.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.620
Some company's and developers say however

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.940
that cheaper leasing rates

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.330
along the 303 make it more
cost effective overall.

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.280
That's especially true when the consumer

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.080
is in or near Phoenix.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.390
- Phoenix for years has
been really a hotbed

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:25.650
for industrial growth,

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.296
but I think what's namely different today

00:03:28.296 --> 00:03:29.700
is these global brands, these
well known consumer brands

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.200
need to service the Southwest.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.480
It's the growth belt, it's
where people are moving to.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.030
- [Narrator] Phoenix has a
population of nearly 5 million

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.000
and like other Sun Belt cities,
it's been growing rapidly

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.922
but it still pales in
comparison to the 24 million

00:03:41.922 --> 00:03:44.370
person market in Southern California.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.510
- The reality is though many

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:47.910
of these products now
are serving the Sun Belt.

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.520
So they're brought in,
whether it's through the port

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.020
of LA Long Beach into Phoenix,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.690
and now you can service
West Texas, Colorado,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.030
New Mexico, Nevada,
all from a central hub.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.430
- You can be either close to the port

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.930
or close to the customer.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.420
So I think with the investment in Phoenix,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.290
we can actually serve
for a certain area both.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.940
- [Narrator] So given
the advantages companies

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.410
and developers see in Phoenix,

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:12.750
some hope it'll be the next Inland Empire,

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.690
but for now it still has a long way to go.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.940
- What I envision over
the next 30 to 40 years

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.880
is you're gonna see a shift
westward closer into California,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:21.840
but still be in Arizona,

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.420
and servicing the Southern
California basin from here.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:25.800
- [Narrator] Currently the Inland Empire

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.050
has one and a half times
the industrial space

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.850
of the entire Phoenix market

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.700
and Loop 303 is just one part of that.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.700
But this highway does
still have a role to play.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:38.700
- I think Loop 303 is
exactly the kind of solution

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.170
companies are looking for

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.310
with the end goal of helping all of us

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.390
get that little package on your doorstep

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.190
or pick up whatever it is at the store

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:49.071
that much quicker and faster and cheaper.

00:04:49.071 --> 00:04:51.738
(mellow music)

